1
00:00:00,120 --> 00:00:01,980
When the game gets tough, we often ask

2
00:00:02,020 --> 00:00:03,760
our team to run faster.

3
00:00:03,770 --> 00:00:06,140
More meetings, more reports, more

4
00:00:06,200 --> 00:00:06,700
urgency.

5
00:00:07,460 --> 00:00:09,600
But what if speed isn't the problem?

6
00:00:09,640 --> 00:00:10,950
What if everyone is running towards a

7
00:00:10,960 --> 00:00:11,610
different goal?

8
00:00:12,320 --> 00:00:14,400
At a mobility cooperative, we introduced

9
00:00:14,460 --> 00:00:16,490
a new tool for handling customer issues.

10
00:00:17,160 --> 00:00:19,620
On paper, it was a major step forward.

11
00:00:20,170 --> 00:00:22,580
In practice, the same inquiry still

12
00:00:22,620 --> 00:00:24,340
traveled from one desk to the next.

13
00:00:25,020 --> 00:00:27,140
We had changed the screen, but not the

14
00:00:27,160 --> 00:00:28,260
way we made decisions.

15
00:00:28,960 --> 00:00:30,760
One morning, a colleague asked a simple

16
00:00:30,840 --> 00:00:33,100
question: "Who can tell the customer

17
00:00:33,140 --> 00:00:35,190
what we're going to do without asking

18
00:00:35,220 --> 00:00:37,760
three people for permission?" Nobody

19
00:00:37,840 --> 00:00:38,700
answered.

20
00:00:38,740 --> 00:00:39,500
That was the problem.

21
00:00:40,680 --> 00:00:42,200
Before adding another feature, we

22
00:00:42,240 --> 00:00:43,140
brought the team together.

23
00:00:43,900 --> 00:00:45,180
We agreed what each person could

24
00:00:45,200 --> 00:00:48,400
resolve, when they needed help, and who

25
00:00:48,420 --> 00:00:49,420
would own the next step.

26
00:00:50,220 --> 00:00:51,680
Then we tested that agreement on just

27
00:00:51,760 --> 00:00:52,900
one type of inquiry.

28
00:00:53,740 --> 00:00:55,340
We listened to what wasn't working and

29
00:00:55,400 --> 00:00:56,940
improved it together.

30
00:00:56,980 --> 00:00:59,920
For example, imagine a request sitting

31
00:00:59,960 --> 00:01:02,410
unanswered because nobody knows whether

32
00:01:02,420 --> 00:01:04,620
they can offer an alternative.

33
00:01:04,680 --> 00:01:05,890
The change might be as simple as

34
00:01:05,920 --> 00:01:07,620
agreeing which options the person

35
00:01:07,680 --> 00:01:09,940
helping the customer can suggest and

36
00:01:09,960 --> 00:01:12,120
when they should ask for advice.

37
00:01:12,180 --> 00:01:13,990
For one week, we observe what happens.

38
00:01:14,680 --> 00:01:16,120
Does the customer receive a useful

39
00:01:16,200 --> 00:01:16,480
answer?

40
00:01:17,130 --> 00:01:18,640
Does the team understand the agreement?

41
00:01:19,260 --> 00:01:20,620
And where do questions arise?

42
00:01:21,800 --> 00:01:23,420
Then we discuss those cases together.

43
00:01:24,180 --> 00:01:25,980
That gives us a concrete experience to

44
00:01:26,040 --> 00:01:28,330
improve the process instead of another

45
00:01:28,360 --> 00:01:28,870
assumption.

46
00:01:29,810 --> 00:01:31,540
That's innovation too.

47
00:01:31,620 --> 00:01:33,240
Sometimes progress doesn't begin with

48
00:01:33,280 --> 00:01:34,580
new technology.

49
00:01:34,600 --> 00:01:36,350
It begins with clear responsibility.

50
00:01:37,200 --> 00:01:38,660
Tomorrow, before asking people to work

51
00:01:38,700 --> 00:01:40,820
harder, try three things.

52
00:01:41,920 --> 00:01:43,240
Ask where the work gets stuck.

53
00:01:43,960 --> 00:01:45,650
Agree on one decision the team can make,

54
00:01:46,360 --> 00:01:47,640
and set a time to review what you've

55
00:01:47,680 --> 00:01:48,020
learned.

56
00:01:48,980 --> 00:01:50,180
You don't have to win the whole game

57
00:01:50,240 --> 00:01:50,580
today.

58
00:01:51,280 --> 00:01:52,740
You need to know what the next inch is,

59
00:01:53,320 --> 00:01:55,200
who will cover it, and how you'll

60
00:01:55,260 --> 00:01:55,980
support each other.
